<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sansoen Phra Barami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">tune chosen <a href="/years/1871">1871</a>; sheet music printed <a href="/years/1888">1888</a>; words <a href="/years/1886">1886</a>, revised <a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as สรรเสริญพระบารมี</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Sansoen Phra Barami&quot; (&quot;Praise the King&quot;) is the royal anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and before 1932 it served as the de facto national anthem of Siam. Its history begins in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1851 two former British officers, Captain Impey and Lieutenant Thomas George Knox, trained the troops of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> and the Second King Pinklao, and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> as honour music for the king of Siam. Phraya Srisunthonwohan later wrote Thai words for it, named Chom Rat Chong Charoen, meaning long live the great king. After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1871, Siam needed a tune of its own, since it was using the same anthem as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Court musicians chose Bulan Loi Luean, a royal composition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, and Chulalongkorn ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> bandmaster Christopher Hewetson to arrange it for military band.</p><p>In 1888 a sheet music of the Siamese national anthem, arranged by the Russian composer Pyotr Schurovsky, was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with the main melody of the present anthem. According to Sugree Charoensook, Schurovsky composed its music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Prince Narisara Nuvadtivong wrote various lyrics for the tune, for the army, for schools and for traditional music bands, and Prince Abhakara Kiartivongse wrote a version for the navy. The current words were written by Narisara in 1886 and revised by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajiravudh">Vajiravudh</a> in 1914, when he set aside all the earlier versions. They were first used by the Wild Tiger Corps and Siamese scouts on 22 January 1914 at Ban Bo Suphan, and the Ministry of Education adopted them in schools nationwide on 1 March of that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The anthem was the de facto national anthem of Siam from 1888 until 1932, when it was replaced by Phleng Chat Siam after the Siamese Revolution. It remained the royal anthem, played to honour the king. In 1935 the melodies of the royal and national anthems were shortened for ceremonial honours in Western style. In 1940 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a> issued the eighth cultural mandate, shortening the words and replacing Siam with Thai; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> these versions were quietly abandoned as unpopular, and the version of 1914 returned.</p><p>The anthem is performed on state occasions and when a high-ranking member of the royal family is present, and it is played before films in cinemas and before many live performances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It was first recorded on an Edison wax cylinder by the ethnomusicologist Carl Stumpf in 1900, performed by a Siamese theatre group visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sansoen Phra Barami” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
